  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1206 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nRainfall Tonight:\n\nA surface frontal boundary evident in backing surface wind \nobservations from northern Minnesota to north Dakota and \nsouthwest Minnesota to central South Dakota gradually progresses\neast today from an impending upper level trough sagging \nsoutheast on GOES water vapor imagery. Eventual fropa tonight \ninto Sunday morning will increase local precipitation chances \ninitially this evening, and through the overnight into Sunday \nmorning. As the upstream trough slowly trudges east today, \nconfluence of the upstream synoptic pattern enhances moisture \nflux convergence towards the local forecast area with 100% \n(GEFS/EPS) probabilities for 1\" PWATs (90+ percentile - SPC RAOB\nClimatology) as integrated vapor transport nears 400 kg m-1 \ns-1. Resultant rainfall will be temporarily heavy at times \novernight into Sunday morning, providing a narrow potential band\nof 1\"-1.25\" rainfall amounts from northeast Iowa through \nsoutheast Minnesota into southwest and central Wisconsin. A \nwidespread 0.25\" - 0.75\" expected outside of this band locally. \n\nMore Seasonable Cold Through Start of Next Week:\n\nStrong low level CAA (15-20C @ 850mb / 24hrs) in the wake of \nthe frontal boundary causes a swing from anomalously warm 90th \npercentile temperatures to 25th percentile (SPC RAOB Climatology\nat MPX) tonight through Sunday. Steepened low level level lapse\nrates will cause strong northwest winds with gusts 25-35 mph \nthrough Sunday. In other words, due to strong winds and WAA \ntonight keeping overnight minimum apparent temperatures to \nremain in the 50s through Saturday morning, the local forecast \narea will experience a 40 degree decrease over the next 48 hours\nas apparent temperatures dip into the teens early Monday \nmorning.",
